`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["AIME: MathArena deprecated final-answer contests ",e.jsx(s,{id:"matharena-leaderboard",children:"MathArena, n.d."})]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["Terminal-Bench 3.0: a 4.9-point gap became 12.7 ",e.jsx(s,{id:"tbench30-2026",children:"Marten et al., 2026a"})]}),`
`]}),e.jsxs(i,{title:"Repeated optimization uses up a benchmark's headroom",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Widely optimized public benchmarks often follow a four-stage cycle, shown in the figure from left to right. A benchmark is released as a hard, clearly specified test. The community climbs it. Repeated optimization compresses the headroom until top scores bunch near the ceiling. A new test then takes its place, as MMLU-Pro followed MMLU ",e.jsx(s,{id:"wang2024mmlupro",children:"Wang et al., 2024"}),"."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:['The statuses below are as of October 2026. Artificial Analysis maintains an Intelligence Index, a weighted average over a set of benchmarks. In September 2026 its version 4.2 removed GPQA Diamond, "an exceptional scientific reasoning evaluation that has now been saturated" ',e.jsx(s,{id:"aa-index-v42",children:"Artificial Analysis, 2026b"}),". GPQA was built to be hard: PhD experts answered about 65% of the full set correctly ",e.jsx(s,{id:"rein2023gpqa",children:"Rein et al., 2023"}),". MathArena, which evaluates models on new math competitions ",e.jsx(s,{id:"matharena2025",children:"Balunović et al., 2025"}),", now lists final-answer competitions as a deprecated category, the American Invitational Mathematics Examination (AIME) 2026 among them ",e.jsx(s,{id:"matharena-leaderboard",children:"MathArena, n.d."}),'. Its maintainers concluded that "final-answer competition problems are no longer an effective source of hard benchmark tasks for frontier models" ',e.jsx(s,{id:"matharena-nofa2026",children:"Sun et al., 2026"}),". A saturated benchmark stops separating frontier models, while it can still measure models far below its ceiling."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The last stage restores the resolution. On Terminal-Bench 2.1, the best of the compared models, Fable 5 in Claude Code, scored 83.8%. Fable 5 and Opus 4.8, both in Claude Code, were 4.9 points apart on 2.1 and 12.7 points apart on the harder 3.0, released on 2026-07-30 with 74 new tasks ",e.jsx(s,{id:"tbench30-2026",children:"Marten et al., 2026a"}),". On a saturated benchmark, a large difference in capability shows up as a difference of a few tasks. Harder tasks bring the scores back into the range where the benchmark can resolve differences."]})]})]}),e.jsxs(a,{optional:!0,title:"Five questions take any benchmark apart",accent:3,number:"44",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Format, scoring, setup, failure modes, status"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["CritPt: test answers private, on a grading server ",e.jsx(s,{id:"critpt2025",children:"Zhu et al., 2025"})]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"One of CritPt's 70 test items moves the score 1.4 points"}),`
`]}),e.jsx(f,{}),e.jsxs(i,{title:"Format, scoring, setup, failure modes and status",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["A benchmark score compresses many choices into one number. Five questions take it apart. ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Format"})," asks what an item looks like and what the model must produce. ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Scoring"})," asks how the output becomes a number. ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Setup"})," asks what surrounds the model: the examples in the prompt, the template, the tools, the time limit and the number of trials. ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Failure modes"})," ask what can make the number wrong without anyone cheating, such as fragile answer extraction, contamination, too few items or the agent's scaffold. ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Status"})," asks where the benchmark stands today: current, saturated at the frontier or retired from a leaderboard. A status always carries a date."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The widget applies the template to MMLU-Pro, CritPt and Terminal-Bench 4.0. Each tab shows one item, which answers the format question, and four chips for the other questions. Compare the chips row by row. Scoring moves from a letter that a regular expression extracts from generated chain of thought, to a grading server, to tests in a verifier container. The course wrote every displayed item in its benchmark's format. None is copied from a benchmark."}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["CritPt (Complex Research using Integrated Thinking, Physics Test) measures reasoning on unpublished, research-level physics problems, in 71 challenges ",e.jsx(s,{id:"critpt2025",children:"Zhu et al., 2025"}),'. A grading server checks every answer: numbers, SymPy expressions and Python functions. The authors publish one example challenge. The solutions to the other 70, the test set, "are kept private to avoid potential contamination" ',e.jsx(s,{id:"critpt2025",children:"Zhu et al., 2025"}),". Answers that were never published cannot appear in training data. A small set has a price: in an evaluator with n items, one item is worth 100/n percentage points, about 1.4 points for CritPt's 70 challenges. Artificial Analysis lists CritPt with a private test set in its index v4.3 ",e.jsx(s,{id:"aa-index-v43",children:"Artificial Analysis, 2026c"}),"."]})]})]}),e.jsxs(a,{optional:!0,title:"Terminal-Bench 4.0: an agent, a container, a verifier",accent:7,number:"45",items:"4",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Multi-step tasks in a sandboxed terminal"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Tests run in a separate verifier container"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Agent = model + scaffold"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["Version 4.0: 66 tasks, 8 removed, 19 fixed ",e.jsx(s,{id:"tbench40-2026",children:"Marten, 2026"})]}),`
`]}),e.jsxs(i,{title:"A Terminal-Bench score belongs to a model, an agent and a version",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Terminal-Bench gives an agent a multi-step task in a sandboxed terminal. The agent works in its own container until it finishes or times out. The task's tests then run in a separate verifier container, which checks the end state the agent left behind. No judge model is involved. Keeping the two containers apart blocks many ways of hacking the reward ",e.jsx(s,{id:"tbench30-2026",children:"Marten et al., 2026a"}),"."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The benchmark measures an agent, and an ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"agent = model + scaffold"}),". The scaffold is the program around the model, such as Claude Code or Codex. It decides what the model sees, which tools it may call and when it stops. A score therefore mixes the model's capability with the scaffold's, unless the scaffold is held fixed and reported. Leaderboard submissions must use the default resources, run at least five trials per task, and score an errored trial as 0 ",e.jsx(s,{id:"tbench-leaderboard",children:"Terminal-Bench Team, n.d."}),"."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Version 4.0, released on 2026-08-28, removed 8 tasks, among them saturated ones, ones models refused and ones with public solutions ",e.jsx(s,{id:"tbench40-2026",children:"Marten, 2026"}),". It fixed 19 tasks and gave every task a flat agent timeout of 8 hours. The ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"v4.0.0"})," release holds 66 tasks ",e.jsx(s,{id:"tbench-repo-v400",children:"Marten et al., 2026b"}),"."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The version decides what a score means. Version 2.1 fixed 28 of the 89 tasks of version 2.0. In the maintainers' paired comparison, Claude Code with Opus 4.6 gained 12.1 points with no change to the model ",e.jsx(s,{id:"tbench21-2026",children:"Terminal-Bench Team, 2026"}),". Without the version number, you cannot tell whether the model or the benchmark moved a score."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["On the 4.0 leaderboard in October 2026, the top entry is Opus 5.5 in Claude Code at maximum effort, with 64.8% ± 3.1 ",e.jsx(s,{id:"tbench-leaderboard",children:"Terminal-Bench Team, n.d."}),". The best agent still fails about a third of its trials, so version 4.0 is not saturated."]})]})]}),e.jsxs(a,{optional:!0,title:"Six more benchmarks, October 2026",accent:0,number:"46",children:[e.jsxs(t.table,{children:[e.jsx(t.thead,{children:e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Benchmark"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Measures"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Scoring"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Test set"})]})}),e.jsxs(t.tbody,{children:[e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsxs(t.td,{children:["Humanity's Last Exam (HLE) ",e.jsx(s,{id:"hle2026nature",children:"Phan et al., 2026"})]}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Expert questions across fields"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Exact answer"}),e.jsxs(t.td,{children:["Public, 2,500 ",e.jsx(s,{id:"hle-site",children:"Center for AI Safety & Scale AI, n.d."})]})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsxs(t.td,{children:["DeepSWE v1.1 ",e.jsx(s,{id:"deepswe2026",children:"Datacurve, 2026"})]}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Long-horizon software engineering"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Behavioral tests"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Public, newly written tasks"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsxs(t.td,{children:["AutomationBench ",e.jsx(s,{id:"automationbench2026",children:"Shepard & Salimans, 2026"})]}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Business workflows across apps"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Final-state checks"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Held-out, 657"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsxs(t.td,{children:["GDPval-AA ",e.jsx(s,{id:"aa-gdpval-aa",children:"Artificial Analysis, n.d.-a"})]}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Professional deliverables"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"LLM-judge panel, Elo"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Public, 220"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsxs(t.td,{children:["PostTrainBench ",e.jsx(s,{id:"posttrainbench2026",children:"Rank et al., 2026"})]}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Agents doing post-training"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Benchmark gains of the trained model"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Public"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsxs(t.td,{children:["Arena ",e.jsx(s,{id:"chiang2024arena",children:"Chiang et al., 2024"})]}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Open-ended chat"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Human votes, Bradley-Terry"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Live"})]})]})]}),e.jsxs(i,{title:"Each current benchmark measures one thing and scores it one way",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Humanity's Last Exam (HLE)"})," collects expert-written questions across many subjects, each with one answer checked automatically ",e.jsx(s,{id:"hle2026nature",children:"Phan et al., 2026"}),". Its 2,500 public questions were finalized on 2025-04-03, and a private set of held-out questions detects overfitting ",e.jsx(s,{id:"hle-site",children:"Center for AI Safety & Scale AI, n.d."}),"."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"DeepSWE v1.1"})," tests coding agents on 113 long-horizon tasks written from scratch ",e.jsx(s,{id:"deepswe2026",children:"Datacurve, 2026"}),". Datacurve states that for this reason no model saw a solution during pretraining. Hand-written verifiers test the software's behavior."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"AutomationBench"}),", from Zapier, asks an agent to carry out business workflows across simulated apps and checks the final state against fixed assertions ",e.jsx(s,{id:"automationbench2026",children:"Shepard & Salimans, 2026"}),". Its leaderboard runs on a held-out set of 657 tasks, and a public set of 600 serves research. Its authors score strict pass or fail: a task counts only if every scored assertion passes. Artificial Analysis runs the held-out set with partial credit for completed objectives, and any guardrail violation sets the task's score to zero ",e.jsx(s,{id:"aa-index-v43",children:"Artificial Analysis, 2026c"}),". A number means little until you know its split and scoring rule."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"GDPval-AA"})," runs models as agents on the 220 public tasks of OpenAI's GDPval, drawn from the work of professionals in 44 occupations ",e.jsx(s,{id:"gdpval2025",children:"Patwardhan et al., 2025"}),". An LLM judge from a panel of three picks the better of two submissions blind, and the results are fitted into Elo ratings ",e.jsx(s,{id:"aa-gdpval-aa",children:"Artificial Analysis, n.d.-a"}),"."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"PostTrainBench"})," asks an agent to post-train a small base model on one H100 GPU in 10 hours and scores the trained model's benchmark gains ",e.jsx(s,{id:"posttrainbench2026",children:"Rank et al., 2026"}),". Some agents trained on test data, downloaded instruction-tuned checkpoints and used credentials they found."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Arena"})," turns human votes between two anonymous answers into ratings with a Bradley-Terry model ",e.jsx(s,{id:"chiang2024arena",children:"Chiang et al., 2024"}),". Its test set is live."]})]})]}),e.jsxs(a,{optional:!0,title:"Nanochat's small-scale fit changes the constant",figure:e.jsx(x,{}),figureWidth:"3/5",accent:7,number:"47",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Same method: fixed FLOPs, swept depth"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Optima at 8.1 to 8.5 tokens per parameter"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["A recipe-specific constant ",e.jsx(s,{id:"karpathy2026nanochat-miniseries",children:"Karpathy, 2026"})]}),`
`]}),e.jsxs(i,{title:"Nanochat's IsoFLOP fits put the optimum near 8 tokens per parameter",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The same fitting procedure can produce a different allocation constant when the recipe and the scale change. Nanochat, a minimal open codebase for training language models, ran the IsoFLOP method on its own recipe ",e.jsx(s,{id:"karpathy2026nanochat-miniseries",children:"Karpathy, 2026"}),". It fixed the compute of each run with its ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"--target_flops"})," option, swept the model depth, and fitted a quadratic to each IsoFLOP U-curve. Across three budgets, the fitted optima used 8.1 to 8.5 tokens per parameter."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The figure plots the three fitted optima against training compute on a log scale, at 1 × 10¹⁸, 3 × 10¹⁸ and 6 × 10¹⁸ FLOPs. The vertical axis is the validation bits per byte, which does not depend on the tokenizer. Lower is better. Each point carries its parameter count, its token count and its ratio of tokens to parameters. The bits per byte fall from 0.9781 at the smallest budget to 0.8874 at the largest."}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The January 2026 maintainer report used one node of eight H100 GPUs for this miniseries. It suggests that Muon, the small model scale or other nanochat specifics could explain the fitted ratio near 8. Chinchilla's roughly 20 tokens per parameter was fitted with a different recipe, so the two numbers do not conflict. A fitted constant holds for the recipe and the budget range it was measured on."})]})]})]}),`
`,e.jsxs(o,{tab:"Summary",plate:"scappi-utensils",children:[e.jsxs(a,{title:"Which model, and how you know",accent:1,number:"48",items:"6",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Fit small runs, validate on a held-out run"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Chinchilla minima: about 20 tokens per parameter"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Serving many tokens: smaller, longer-trained"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Keep sweeps comparable, share the WSD prefix"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Small models: BPB on high-SNR benchmarks"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Report the protocol, run the checklist"}),`
`]}),e.jsxs(i,{title:"Choose the model from fitted small runs, and know it worked from a reproducible measurement",children:[e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The lecture asked one question in two parts: which model should you train with your budget, and how do you know it worked?"}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"Which model."})," Fit small runs, and validate the fit on a held-out run. You sweep model sizes and token counts at budgets far below the final run's, fit a power law in log space, and trust the fit at a larger scale only once it predicts a run it was not fitted to. The IsoFLOP minima give the compute-optimal model size and token count at each budget, which Chinchilla puts at about 20 tokens per parameter for training compute alone ",e.jsx(s,{id:"hoffmann2022",children:"Hoffmann et al., 2022"}),". A model that will serve many tokens pays an inference cost on each of them, so it is trained smaller and longer, past the training-compute optimum, as Llama 3 8B and 70B were. A comparable sweep fixes the data, the architecture rule, the schedule and the measurement, varies N and D, and scales the learning rate and the batch size by a rule declared in advance. WSD makes the sweep cheaper in compute, because every endpoint of one model size shares a single stable prefix and adds only its own short decay branch."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:[e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"How you know."})," You know it worked only through a measurement you can reproduce. While a model is small, its accuracy can stay at chance even as its loss keeps falling. You therefore score the gold answer continuously, in bits per byte, on a benchmark whose signal across models is larger than its noise from checkpoint to checkpoint ",e.jsx(s,{id:"heineman2025signalnoise",children:"Heineman et al., 2025"}),". When you compare models, the protocol is part of the score: the harness and its version, the prompt and the shots, the scoring and the revisions. Report it with every number, and run the consumer's checklist on an evals table before you attribute a difference to the weights. Both halves rest on the same rule: say which quantity you held fixed, and compare only at that."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The exercise runs both halves in miniature. You train about five small model sizes with WSD decay branches, fit the IsoFLOP minima and then N*(C), and predict the loss of a reference model trained with about ten times the compute of your largest run. Then you measure models with lm-eval-harness pinned at one version, under two setup ablations, and record the protocol next to every score."}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Week 9 uses the same controlled loop to decide which data reaches training."})]})]}),e.jsx(a,{title:"To-dos until next week",accent:2,number:"49",children:e.jsxs("div",{className:"flex flex-col gap-10",children:[e.jsx("div",{className:"flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-x-24 gap-y-6",children:e.jsxs("figure",{className:"text-center",children:[e.jsx("a",{href:"https://tally.so/r/0V876Q",children:e.jsx("img",{src:"images/qr-week-07-muddiest-point.svg",alt:"QR code for the muddiest-point survey, tally.so/r/0V876Q",className:"w-56 h-56"})}),e.jsxs("figcaption",{children:[e.jsx("strong",{children:"Muddiest point"}),e.jsx("br",{}),"Do it right now! (2 min)"]})]})}),e.jsxs("div",{className:"grid gap-x-12 gap-y-6 md:grid-cols-3",children:[e.jsxs("div",{children:[e.jsx("div",{className:"font-semibold",children:"Before the tutorial"}),e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
